<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Province of Hohenzollern</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hohenzollern Lands, Hohenzollernsche Lande</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Province of Hohenzollern, officially the Hohenzollern Lands, was a Prussian territory in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> formed in 1850 from the principalities of Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> and Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a>, whose princes had ceded them to King Frederick William IV. An exclave surrounded by Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, it was governed from Sigmaringen. After 1945 it became part of Württemberg-Hohenzollern and in 1952 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/province-of-hohenzollern.jpg" alt="Province of Hohenzollern" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Hohenzollern.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the revolutions of 1848–1849, the two princes ceded their principalities to their kinsman, King Frederick William IV of Prussia, and the territories were transferred in 1850, forming the <b>Prussian Hohenzollern Lands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
